  related={['sssNumber', 'tinNumber', 'philhealthPin', 'pagibigMid']}
  isApp={false}
>
  <Fragment slot="answer">
    <p>
      Under the <strong>First Time Jobseekers Assistance Act (RA 11261)</strong>, a Filipino looking for work for the first time
      gets key pre-employment documents <strong>free</strong> — once, one copy each, within a year. Get a free
      <strong> Barangay Certification</strong> (and sign the Oath of Undertaking) at the barangay where you have lived for at least
      six months, then present the original to each office: <strong>NBI clearance</strong> (normally ₱160),
      <strong>police clearance</strong> (₱150 plus fees), <strong>PSA birth certificate</strong> (₱155), barangay clearance, TIN,
      UMID, a public-hospital medical certificate and public-school records. Passports, the PRC and CSC exams and driver's
      licenses are not free.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <h2>Who Qualifies</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A Filipino citizen</strong> actively looking for a job, in the Philippines or abroad, <strong>for the first time</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>A resident of the barangay for at least six months</strong> (IRR Sec. 4(d)). The 2023 guidelines word it as "a resident of the barangay as of a particular date", but NBI's 2025 Citizen's Charter still asks for six months, so expect it.</li>
    <li><strong>Included:</strong> college, tech-voc and senior high graduates; early school leavers and out-of-school youth; people not in work, education or training; students on leave; students who want to work while enrolled.</li>
    <li><strong>Earlier work that does not count as employment:</strong> running your own business, independent contracting, and government programs such as GIP, SPES and TUPAD.</li>
    <li><strong>Age:</strong> no limit in the law or its rules; 15- to 17-year-olds need a parent's or guardian's consent and may work only under DOLE's rules for minors.</li>
    <li><strong>Not covered:</strong> beneficiaries of the JobStart Program (RA 10869) or of other laws with similar exemptions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Documents You Can Get for Free (and What They Normally Cost)</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>Issued by</th><th>Normal fee</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {documents.map((d) => (
          <tr><td><strong>{d.doc}</strong></td><td>{d.from}</td><td>{d.normal}</td><td>{d.note}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="text-sm text-ink-soft">
    Normal fees as published by <a href={NBI_FTJ} target="_blank" rel="noopener">NBI</a>, <a href={PNP} target="_blank" rel="noopener">PNP</a>
    and <a href={PSA} target="_blank" rel="noopener">PSA Serbilis</a>, checked September 23, 2026. Coverage: RA 11261 Sec. 4, IRR Sec. 5
    and the 2023 Joint Operational Guidelines Sec. II. Getting a TIN is covered on <a href="/how-to-get-tin-number/">How to Get a TIN</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Not Covered: Fees You Still Pay</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>PRC professional licensure exam applications.</li>
    <li>DFA passport applications, and authentication or apostille of documents.</li>
    <li>The CSC Career Service Examination and the LTO driver's license.</li>
    <li>Laboratory tests and procedures needed for a medical certificate.</li>
    <li>Second and later copies, and anything after the one-time benefit is used or the certificate's year runs out.</li>
    <li>Documents from private schools, hospitals and other private offices — the law covers government offices only.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to Get the Barangay Certification</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Go to the barangay hall where you have lived for at least six months.</li>
    <li>Fill out and sign the <a href={FORM2} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Oath of Undertaking</a> before the Punong Barangay or an authorized barangay officer; if you are 15 to 17, a parent or guardian signs the consent part.</li>
    <li>Receive the free <a href={FORM1} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Barangay Certification</a>: an original on the barangay's letterhead with its dry seal, signed, valid one year. The barangay keeps its own copy. Neither form needs notarizing.</li>
    <li>Within the year, bring the original to each office with the IDs it asks for; you get one free copy of each document.</li>
    <li>Tell the barangay once you get a job — it is one of the undertakings in the Oath.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>If an Office Still Charges You</h2>
  <p>
    Complain to the head of that office, who must act within three working days; unresolved cases go to the Inter-Agency
    Monitoring Committee chaired by the DOLE Secretary, which can recommend an administrative case (IRR Sec. 14). RA 11261 has no
    fine or jail term for officials — refusal is an administrative offense — while an applicant who falsifies a certificate is
    liable under the Revised Penal Code. When in doubt, the law says any ambiguity is read in the applicant's favor (Sec. 11).
  </p>

